---
import { CATALOG } from '../../lab/catalog';
import Lab from '../../layouts/Lab.astro';
---

<Lab title="Tests" description="Tests that measure pixi-marrow on the device that opens them.">
    <main class="lab-index">
        <a href="/" class="lab-back">pixi-marrow</a>
        <h1 class="lab-title">Lab</h1>
        <p class="lab-question">
            Each test measures pixi-marrow on the device that opens it, a phone above all. Open one, keep the screen on and wait about a minute. It
            shows its result at the end, with a text to copy.
        </p>
        <ul class="lab-tests">
            {
                CATALOG.map((test) => (
                    <li>
                        <a href={`/lab/${test.name}/`} class="lab-test">
                            <strong>{test.title}</strong>
                            <span>{test.question}</span>
                            <span class="lab-scene">{test.scene(test.count)}</span>
                        </a>
                    </li>
                ))
            }
        </ul>
        <h2 class="lab-subtitle">Settings in the address</h2>
        <ul class="lab-settings">
            <li><code>?renderer=webgpu</code> draws with WebGPU where the browser has it. WebGL2 is the default.</li>
            <li><code>?n=100</code> sets the number of skeletons.</li>
            <li><code>?rounds=5</code> runs every side 5 times. The default is 3.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2 class="lab-subtitle">How it measures</h2>
        <p class="lab-question">
            A frame is the update of every skeleton, the submit of the render and the wait until the GPU has finished it. Nothing waits for the
            screen, so the times are the same on a screen of 60 Hz and of 120 Hz. The sides of a test take turns, so the heat of the phone weighs
            on all of them alike.
        </p>
    </main>
</Lab>
